WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.470 --> 00:00:04.100
- [Narrator] You know,
stubbing your toe hurts.

00:00:04.100 --> 00:00:08.010
It's right up there with
paper cuts and chapped lips,

00:00:08.010 --> 00:00:10.990
annoying minor injuries that hurt way more

00:00:10.990 --> 00:00:13.050
than they have any right to.

00:00:13.050 --> 00:00:15.100
But it turns out there's a good reason

00:00:15.100 --> 00:00:18.683
why stubbing your toe hurts so much.

00:00:19.760 --> 00:00:21.290
When you stub your toe,

00:00:21.290 --> 00:00:22.790
you're slamming it with a force

00:00:22.790 --> 00:00:25.520
equal to two to three
times your body weight.

00:00:25.520 --> 00:00:28.770
That's about the same
force as a karate punch.

00:00:28.770 --> 00:00:31.420
And since your toe has
a tiny surface area,

00:00:31.420 --> 00:00:33.610
that force can't be spread out,

00:00:33.610 --> 00:00:37.080
so the pain stays concentrated
at the point of impact.

00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:39.370
It's the same reason it hurts so much more

00:00:39.370 --> 00:00:41.810
to step on the tiny,
pointy end of a thumbtack

00:00:41.810 --> 00:00:43.960
than the wider, blunt end.

00:00:43.960 --> 00:00:45.940
But you don't just feel
the immediate shock

00:00:45.940 --> 00:00:47.920
like when you step on a thumbtack.

00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:51.040
There's that aching
throb that comes after.

00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:52.950
That's because when you stub your toe,

00:00:52.950 --> 00:00:55.760
you're actually hitting a
bundle of special nerve endings

00:00:55.760 --> 00:00:57.630
called nociceptors.

00:00:57.630 --> 00:01:01.030
They all fire at once,
blaring a danger signal.

00:01:01.030 --> 00:01:04.250
But some signals travel
faster than others.

00:01:04.250 --> 00:01:06.850
The faster A-delta nociceptors

00:01:06.850 --> 00:01:08.920
fire the first wave of signal,

00:01:08.920 --> 00:01:11.220
which races at 20 meters per second

00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:14.520
of thousands of densely
bundled nerve fibers,

00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:16.630
and ultimately to your brain.

00:01:16.630 --> 00:01:19.280
That causes the sharp,
sudden pain you feel

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:21.100
at the moment of impact.

00:01:21.100 --> 00:01:24.090
But some nerve fibers
called C nociceceptors

00:01:24.090 --> 00:01:28.250
send a slower signal at
only 2 meters per second.

00:01:28.250 --> 00:01:29.750
So after a moment's delay,

00:01:29.750 --> 00:01:32.710
the second wave of pain
signals reach your brain.

00:01:32.710 --> 00:01:35.203
That's the dull throbbing that lingers on.

00:01:36.050 --> 00:01:38.520
You can find nociceptors
all over your body

00:01:38.520 --> 00:01:40.500
from your eyes to your bladder.

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:42.600
But they're concentrated
at the highest densities

00:01:42.600 --> 00:01:44.210
in parts of your body that you use

00:01:44.210 --> 00:01:45.630
to explore your environment,

00:01:45.630 --> 00:01:48.280
like your fingertips and your lips.

00:01:48.280 --> 00:01:51.320
That's why accidents like
paper cuts and chapped lips

00:01:51.320 --> 00:01:54.300
can also hurt more than
they seem like they should.

00:01:54.300 --> 00:01:57.420
Now, your toe isn't packed
with as many nociceptors

00:01:57.420 --> 00:01:58.950
as your fingertips,

00:01:58.950 --> 00:02:00.720
but since there's not
much in the way of padding

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:02.210
to cushion the blow,

00:02:02.210 --> 00:02:05.510
it's easy to set those
unprotected nociceptors off.

00:02:05.510 --> 00:02:07.800
And that's no coincidence.

00:02:07.800 --> 00:02:11.040
Researchers suspect the pain
that we feel from mishaps

00:02:11.040 --> 00:02:12.400
like a stubbed toe

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:15.430
might have even saved
our ancestors' lives.

00:02:15.430 --> 00:02:17.010
Back before antibiotics,

00:02:17.010 --> 00:02:20.750
even the tiniest cut could
mean a deadly infection,

00:02:20.750 --> 00:02:23.120
and feet, which were constantly in contact

00:02:23.120 --> 00:02:25.990
with dirty, bacteria-infested surfaces,

00:02:25.990 --> 00:02:28.270
were particularly vulnerable.

00:02:28.270 --> 00:02:30.570
So the people who had extra-sensitive feet

00:02:30.570 --> 00:02:33.440
might have been more careful
about where they stepped.

00:02:33.440 --> 00:02:36.430
As a result, they'd be less
likely to get infections

00:02:36.430 --> 00:02:38.950
and would live to pass on their genes.

00:02:38.950 --> 00:02:41.460
So the next time that
you collapse on the floor

00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:44.120
cradling your aching toe,

00:02:44.120 --> 00:02:47.020
you can thank your
great-great-great-great-great-great-

00:02:47.020 --> 00:02:49.010
great-great-great-great-great-grandpa

00:02:49.010 --> 00:02:49.963
for the privilege.

